From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Scott Quarrier <squarrier@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 5 Oct 2026 20:15:18 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] glm5_next/cuda: kept prompts' states and evicted rows in host
 RAM on the AFD attention node, TF_GLM_KEPT_HOST and TF_GLM_HOST_CACHE_GIB
 (glm53f-afd's host RAM tier @ 91db3cc, design)

Hugh Madden (@dangerm00se, github.com/hughmadden) authored the design this follows, glm53f-afd's host RAM tier, at
glm53f-afd @ 91db3cc (https://github.com/hughmadden/glm53f-afd, MIT License, Copyright (c) 2026 Turquoise Bay AI Pty
Ltd):
crates/glm53f-coordinator/src/hostcache.rs, crates/glm53f-coordinator/src/scheduler.rs and docs/RUNNING.md ("KV
snapshots and the RAM tier"). glm53f-afd's NOTICE credits the tier to Hugh Madden's mimo26f-afd, and its host cache's
eviction design to T.J. Purtell's (@wrldsuksgo2mars, github.com/tpurtell) ds41rt. Only the design is taken; no code
is copied.

On the RTX 5090 the --parallel pool gets what TF_GLM_CACHE_GIB leaves after TF_GLM_CACHE_ENTRIES kept states (a KDA
state and a DFlash2 window each, 0.178 GiB at world 1): at the release config (6.5 GiB, 20 kept prompts) 3.56 GiB of
the 6.5 hold states and the pool is 727,040 tokens.

- families/glm5_next/cuda/kept_host.py (new): TF_GLM_KEPT_HOST=1 moves a kept prompt's KDA states, convolution
  windows and DFlash2 window to a slot of pinned host slabs when it is kept, and back into the stream's slot when a
  request resumes it; the GPU reserves one state (STAGING) for a keep's copies instead of TF_GLM_CACHE_ENTRIES, so the
  pool is 1,261,568 tokens at the release config in the same GPU bytes (TF_GLM_CACHE_GIB=8.5: 1,579,008, glm53f-afd
  v1.0's 1.58M). TF_GLM_HOST_CACHE_GIB=G (with TF_GLM_KEPT_HOST=1): a kept prompt the pool evicts for room parks its
  rows in pinned pages of 2,048 tokens (the rows pool.Arena.copy copies, plane by plane) with its states in their slot;
  a request it prefixes gets a new extent with the rows copied back instead of prefilling them; parked prompts leave
  least recently used first (pages, or TF_GLM_HOST_CACHE_ENTRIES, default 32), never the one being resumed. Every copy
  is exact and runs on the current stream, as the device copies it replaces; a slot or page waits on its last copy's
  event before the next, and a keep's device sources are recorded on the stream.
- multi.py: MultiDecoder builds the tier (None when off; two ranks run without it); _keep stows, _admitted restores
  (states, DFlash2 window, a parked prompt's rows), _resume and _kept_by_id see parked prompts after the GPU's, admit
  places a parked prompt's resume in a new extent and never drops it meanwhile, _evict parks before it drops, _drop
  gives the slot back, /health gains kept_host.
- engine.py: the pool formula reserves kept_host.reserved_states (TF_GLM_CACHE_ENTRIES states, or one with the tier);
  the settings are refused before the load off the AFD attention node, below --parallel 2, and TF_GLM_HOST_CACHE_GIB
  without TF_GLM_KEPT_HOST=1; rank 0 prints the tier's line.

Off (both unset): tier None, the pool formula's value, kept prompts, extents and calls are the ones before. On: the
same replies, prompt chunks and kept points; only how many kept prompts survive, and where their bytes live, change.

Tests: tests/test_glm_kept_host.py (CPU, 12): the switches and refusals; the pool formula (2.0's 727,040 ->
1,261,568 at 6,780 B a token); a kept state's bytes through a slot and back (float32, bf16, the DFlash2 window); a full
tier leaves a state on the GPU; parked rows come back as Arena.copy copies them (four lengths, a shorter cut, nothing
else written); parked prompts leave least recently used first, never the held one, and past the count; the AFD node's
decoder (World1 on the fake model): off and TF_GLM_KEPT_HOST=1 give the same replies, cached counts, kept prompts and
extents, every kept state in a host slot; with TF_GLM_HOST_CACHE_GIB the three evicted prompts resume from host RAM
(999 cached where off prefills) with the same replies; two ranks run without the tier.

Credit: THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md ("GLM kept prompts in host RAM").

